from chemistry_helpers import TOPICS
from chemistry_qa_writer import q,write_bank
items=[
q(1,'What type of bond forms when non-metal atoms share electrons?','Covalent.','4.2.1.4'),
q(1,'State the charge on an oxide ion.','2−.','4.2.1.2'),
q(1,'What does the state symbol (aq) mean?','Dissolved in water.','4.2.2.2'),
q(1,'Name the carbon structure that is a single layer of graphite.','Graphene.','4.2.3.3'),
q(1,'State the size range of nanoparticles.','1–100 nm.','4.2.4.1'),
q(2,'Explain what an ionic bond is.','It is a **strong electrostatic attraction** between **oppositely charged ions**.','4.2.1.2–4.2.1.3','2022 Q01.6–01.7'),
q(2,'Explain why magnesium chloride has the formula MgCl₂.','- Magnesium forms **Mg²⁺** and chlorine forms **Cl⁻**.\n- Two chloride ions balance one magnesium ion, making the compound **electrically neutral**.','4.2.1.3'),
q(2,'Explain why a metal conducts electricity.','- It has **delocalised electrons that can move** through the structure.\n- These electrons **carry charge**.','4.2.2.8','2022 Q01.5; 2023 Q06.2'),
q(2,'Why does solid sodium chloride not conduct electricity?','- Its ions are **held in fixed positions** in the lattice.\n- There are no **mobile charged particles** to carry current through it.','4.2.2.3'),
q(2,'Explain why oxygen has a low boiling point.','- There are **weak intermolecular forces** between oxygen molecules.\n- Little energy is needed to overcome them; the covalent bonds inside the molecules remain intact.','4.2.2.4','2019 Q05.3'),
q(2,'Explain why graphite can be used as a lubricant.','- There are **weak forces between its layers**.\n- The layers **slide over one another** easily.','4.2.3.2'),
q(2,'A substance melts at −20 °C and boils at 60 °C. Give its state at 25 °C and explain your choice.','- Liquid.\n- 25 °C is **above its melting point but below its boiling point**.','4.2.2.1'),
q(2,'Give two limitations of a ball-and-stick molecular model.','- Atoms and gaps may **not be to scale**.\n- Bonds are represented by rods, but real bonds are **attractions involving electrons and nuclei**, not physical sticks.','4.2.1.4','2019 Q05.2'),
q(2,'Why might a nanoparticle catalyst work effectively using less material than a larger-particle catalyst?','- Nanoparticles have a **larger surface area to volume ratio**.\n- A larger fraction of the material is exposed at the surface where reaction occurs.','4.2.4.1–4.2.4.2','2023 Q10.1'),
q(3,'Explain why sodium chloride has a high melting point but conducts electricity when molten.','- A **giant ionic lattice** contains strong electrostatic attractions between opposite ions.\n- Much energy is needed to **overcome these attractions**.\n- When molten, the **ions can move and carry charge**.','4.2.2.3'),
q(3,'Explain why an alloy is often harder than the pure metal.','- Atoms of the added elements are **different sizes**.\n- They **distort the regular layers** of metal atoms.\n- The layers **cannot slide as easily**, so the alloy resists deformation.','4.2.2.7','2023 Q06.3'),
q(3,'Explain why diamond is hard and does not conduct electricity.','- Each carbon forms **four strong covalent bonds** in a rigid giant structure.\n- These bonds resist deformation, making it **very hard**.\n- It has **no delocalised electrons or mobile ions** to carry charge.','4.2.3.1','2022 Q03.1–03.2'),
q(3,'Explain why graphite has a high melting point and conducts electricity.','- Each carbon forms **three strong covalent bonds** within a giant layered structure.\n- Much energy is needed to break these bonds.\n- Each carbon contributes a **delocalised electron**, which can move and carry charge.','4.2.3.2','2019 Q03.1'),
q(3,'Explain why a polymer can be solid at room temperature while a small molecule such as methane is a gas.','- Polymers consist of **very large, long molecules**.\n- Forces **between polymer molecules are stronger** than those between small methane molecules.\n- More energy is needed to overcome those forces; strong covalent bonds within either molecule are not broken in melting or boiling.','4.2.2.4–4.2.2.5','2023 Q04.5'),
q(3,'A cubic nanoparticle has side length 20 nm. Calculate its surface area, volume and surface area to volume ratio.','- Surface area = 6 × 20² = **2,400 nm²**.\n- Volume = 20³ = **8,000 nm³**.\n- SA/V = 2,400 ÷ 8,000 = **0.30 nm⁻¹**.','4.2.4.1','2019 Q03.3'),
q(3,'Describe three ways the particle model differs from real substances.','- Particles are not all identical hard spheres: their sizes and structures differ.\n- Simple drawings often omit **attractive forces** between particles.\n- A still drawing does not show **continuous particle movement**.','4.2.2.1'),
q(4,'Describe how magnesium and oxygen form magnesium oxide, including the electron changes and the resulting bonding.','- A magnesium atom **loses two electrons**, becoming Mg²⁺.\n- An oxygen atom **gains two electrons**, becoming O²⁻.\n- Both ions have **full outer shells**.\n- **Electrostatic attraction between opposite ions** holds a giant ionic lattice together.','4.2.1.2–4.2.1.3','2022 Q01.7'),
q(4,'Describe the outer-shell electrons in a dot-and-cross diagram of hydrogen chloride. Explain what holds the atoms together.','- Show **one shared pair**, containing one electron from hydrogen and one from chlorine.\n- Chlorine also has **three lone pairs**.\n- Hydrogen has two electrons in its shell; chlorine has eight in its outer shell when the shared pair is counted.\n- Both positive nuclei **attract the shared negative electrons**, making a covalent bond.','4.2.1.4','2023 Q04.4'),
q(4,'Compare particle arrangement and movement in a solid and a gas, and explain what changes on heating.','- In a solid, particles are **close together in fixed positions**.\n- They **vibrate**; in a gas, particles are far apart and move randomly in all directions.\n- Heating transfers energy to the substance.\n- During a change of state, energy overcomes attractions and changes particle arrangement; the particles themselves do not expand or turn into different substances.','4.2.2.1'),
q(4,'Give two uses of fullerenes or nanotubes and explain the useful property for each.','- A hollow fullerene cage can **carry drug molecules**, making targeted drug delivery possible.\n- Carbon nanotubes have **high tensile strength**, so adding them can strengthen composite materials.\n- Their useful properties arise from their carbon structures, not simply from being small.','4.2.3.3','2022 Q03.3–03.4'),
q(6,'Compare diamond, sodium chloride and methane in terms of structure, melting point and electrical conductivity.','- Diamond is **giant covalent**; many strong covalent bonds require much energy to break, giving a high melting point.\n- It lacks mobile ions and delocalised electrons, so it does not conduct.\n- Sodium chloride has a **giant ionic lattice**; strong attractions between opposite ions give a high melting point.\n- It conducts when molten or dissolved because ions can move, but not when solid.\n- Methane consists of **small molecules**; weak intermolecular forces need little energy to overcome, so its melting point is low.\n- Methane has no mobile ions or delocalised electrons, so it does not conduct.','4.2.2.3–4.2.2.6; 4.2.3.1','2019 Q05.3; 2022 Q03.1–03.2'),
q(6,'A company plans to use silver nanoparticles in an antibacterial fabric. Evaluate the decision and the evidence it should obtain.','- A **large surface area to volume ratio** can make a small amount effective at contacting bacteria.\n- An antibacterial fabric could reduce bacterial growth and odours, but the effectiveness must be measured rather than assumed.\n- Compare treated and untreated fabric under the same conditions, including after washing.\n- Investigate whether nanoparticles are released during use or washing and whether people or organisms are exposed.\n- Assess toxicity at realistic **doses and exposure routes**, including longer-term effects.\n- Use the fabric if evidence supports a useful benefit with acceptable exposure and environmental risks; uncertain safety cannot be settled just by noting that bulk silver is familiar.','4.2.4.2','2019 Q03.4; 2023 Q10.1')]
write_bank(TOPICS[1],'Bonding, structure and properties of matter',items,'- Electrical conductivity and metal/alloy structure: 2022 Q01.5; 2023 Q06.2–06.3.\n- Covalent diagrams and molecular properties: 2019 Q05.1–05.3; 2023 Q04.4–04.5.\n- Carbon structures: 2019 Q03.1; 2022 Q03.1–03.4.\n- Nanoparticles: 2019 Q03.3–03.4; 2023 Q10.1.\n\nThese are recurring themes in the sampled papers. Other prompts extend coverage of the current specification. Wording, values and mark allocations are original.')
